        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Paver sealer turns white</strong> because water got trapped under a film that cannot let it out. Confirm what you are looking at first — blushing is cloudiness inside the film, efflorescence is powder that rubs off — because the fixes are completely different.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A solvent-based acrylic usually clears with a careful <strong class="text-white">xylene re-melt on a hot dry day</strong>. A water-based one needs stripping and a fresh start. And in either case, the prevention is the same: dry pavers, dry joints, two thin coats, and a morning start with a clear forecast ahead of it.
          </p>
